        "descriptions": [
          {
            "lang": "en-Us",
            "description": "With the rise of the Internet and social media platforms, people now have access to more weather information than ever before. Although this allows end users to receive weather information from a variety of sources, the sheer volume of available information likely contributes to a perception that weather risk messages are inconsistent. Members of the weather enterprise share these concerns and believe that these perceived inconsistencies may negatively affect their many audiences. The challenge, however, is that there are only a few studies to-date that have explored message consistency in a weather context. Therefore, in its current state, the weather enterprise lacks empirical evidence that (1) demonstrates that message consistency is a relevant operational concern and (2) provides research-guided recommendations to practitioners and operational meteorologists on how to achieve a more consistent message. To address this operational need, this research project conceptualized ‘message consistency’ for the weather enterprise and employs a social science mixed-methods approach to explore the effects of weather-related graphical inconsistencies on lay public end users. In particular, this project used the Storm Prediction Center’s (SPC) Convective Outlook graphic as a vehicle to investigate the role that visual design plays in keeping a weather-related message consistent.",
            "descriptionType": "Abstract"
          },
          {
            "lang": "en-Us",
            "description": "The third mission provided some evidence that confirmed findings from the first two missions about message consistency, and at the same time, put forward new findings that deserve further research and development. As a result, this mission aimed to overcome some of the limitations of the third mission by (1) using a simpler experimental design, (2) developing Convective Outlook graphics that were devoid of a geographical location, and (3) using a representative sample of the U.S.\npublic to increase the generalizability of the study’s findings about message consistency.\n\nWhereas the previous mission used a factorial experimental design, this mission used a single factor experimental design to manipulate the risk gap between two Convective Outlook graphics. After the last mission determined that participants viewed being on a risk boundary as half-way between two risk categories, we decided to combine these manipulations in this mission. This resulted in five experimental conditions exploring the risk gap (or the difference between the risk category of two different maps) between two Convective Outlook graphics. In addition to these five experimental conditions exploring the risk gap, we also included a single experimental condition for color differences. This condition was compared to the uniform risk gap condition, as this was the control condition. Therefore, this mission employed a 5 (risk gap) x 1 (color scheme) single factor, randomized experimental design. This resulted in a total of 6 experimental conditions.\n\nLike the Convective Outlook graphics created in the second and third mission, this study started with the same PowerPoint template to generate graphics for each experimental condition. However, in order to explore the effects of inconsistent information among a more representative sample of the U.S. public, the research team had to remove all geographic references from the graphics. This was done to make it more realistic that someone in both Georgia and Oklahoma could look at the same graphics and answer the survey questions given the same hypothetical scenario. Therefore, to create these new graphics, the research team zoomed to the county level on the PowerPoint template and marked a position on the graphic with a star that read “Point A.\" The two hypothetical weather sources used in the third mission were also used in this one (i.e., The Weather Authority and The Weather Experts).\n\nThe survey instrument used a hypothetical scenario that informed participants that meteorologists were forecasting a threat of severe weather for their area on a Saturday afternoon. After reading the scenario, participants were randomly assigned to one of the 6 experimental conditions. However, instead of showing both a reference graphic and a\nmanipulated graphic at the same time, Study 4 used a tiered approach to first show people the reference graphic (i.e. First Graphic) and the hypothetical scenario described above. Then, after 18 seconds had passed, participants were given the option to continue to the next survey page. Participants were then told that they decided to search for more information and shown the same reference graphic (i.e., First Graphic). After 9 seconds, the second graphic from another\nsource appeared on the screen next to the first graphic. This new methodology was used to increase external validity and more closely mimic the process of searching for additional weather information—as people are not often shown information from two sources at the same time in a real-world setting\n\nBecause Convective Outlook graphics do not provide any efficacy information, this study also showed participants an efficacy message after seeing the two Convective Outlook graphics. This intervention was used to determine whether providing efficacy information on Convective Outlook graphics might be beneficial for members of the public. This was followed by measures of perceived consistency, perceived susceptibility, perceived severity, uncertainty discrepancy, anxiety about uncertainty discrepancy, self-efficacy, response efficacy, and behavioral intentions to seek information and perform protective action(s).",
            "descriptionType": "Abstract"
          }
        ],
